{
  "concept": "A batted ball's flight is a parabola in the distance–height plane, so whether it clears an outfield wall is simply a comparison of the curve's height with the wall's height at one particular distance.",
  "figureType": "line_plot",
  "elements": [
    "Horizontal axis labeled 'Distance (ft)' from 0 to 600 with ticks and labels every 100 ft, arrowhead at right end",
    "Vertical axis labeled 'Height (ft)' from 0 to about 320 with labeled ticks at 100, 200, 300, arrowhead at top",
    "Faint dotted gridlines at every 100 ft horizontally and every 50 ft vertically",
    "Teal parabolic trajectory curve starting at the origin, peaking slightly above 150 ft near 290 ft, landing just past 560 ft",
    "Small teal arrowheads spaced along the curve indicating direction of travel",
    "Small open circular marker on the rising branch of the curve near 200 ft",
    "Leader line and label 'Trajectory of ball' pointing to that circular marker",
    "Leader line and label 'Position of hitter' pointing to the origin",
    "Short red rectangle standing on the x-axis at 400 ft representing the outfield wall, with label 'Outfield wall'",
    "Draggable distance marker dot on the curve with dashed vertical drop line to the x-axis and dashed horizontal line to the y-axis",
    "Height readout callout near the marker showing (distance, height)",
    "Clearance annotation comparing ball height to wall height at the wall's distance (green 'CLEARS' / red 'HITS WALL' badge)",
    "Sampled-point view: discrete teal dots at regular distance intervals along the same arc, with a small side table of (distance, height) values"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "distanceAlongPath",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Distance from hitter (ft)",
      "range": [0, 570, 5],
      "default": 200,
      "teaches": "Each horizontal distance corresponds to exactly one height on the parabola; the ball rises, peaks near 290 ft, then falls."
    },
    {
      "id": "wallPosition",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Outfield wall distance (ft)",
      "range": [100, 580, 10],
      "default": 400,
      "teaches": "Clearing the fence is a comparison of two heights at a single x-value, so a wall far enough out is eventually not cleared."
    },
    {
      "id": "wallHeight",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Wall height (ft)",
      "range": [5, 60, 1],
      "default": 12,
      "teaches": "The barrier's own height matters as much as its distance — the home run condition is h(x_wall) > wall height."
    },
    {
      "id": "viewMode",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Display",
      "options": ["Smooth path", "Sampled points"],
      "default": "Smooth path",
      "teaches": "The continuous curve and a table of discrete (distance, height) samples describe the same motion."
    },
    {
      "id": "flight",
      "type": "animation",
      "label": "Play ball flight",
      "loop": false,
      "teaches": "The static parabola is a record of motion in time; the ball traverses the arc from hitter to landing point."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The arc of the hit",
      "narration": "A batted ball leaves the hitter at the origin and traces a downward-opening parabola, peaking a little above 150 ft near 290 ft before landing past 560 ft. Watch the ball travel the whole path.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Smooth path", "distanceAlongPath": 200, "wallPosition": 400, "wallHeight": 12 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Reading a height at one distance",
      "narration": "Drag the marker to 200 ft: the dashed drop line ties that horizontal distance to a single height of about 140 ft. Every distance on the axis has exactly one height on the curve.",
      "state": { "distanceAlongPath": 200, "viewMode": "Smooth path", "wallPosition": 400, "wallHeight": 12 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Find the peak",
      "narration": "Slide out to about 290 ft, where the height stops increasing. This vertex is the maximum height of the flight — beyond it the ball is descending.",
      "state": { "distanceAlongPath": 290, "viewMode": "Smooth path", "wallPosition": 400, "wallHeight": 12 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Does it clear the wall?",
      "narration": "The wall stands at 400 ft. Move the marker there and compare: the ball is roughly 140 ft up while the fence is only 12 ft — an easy home run.",
      "state": { "distanceAlongPath": 400, "wallPosition": 400, "wallHeight": 12, "viewMode": "Smooth path" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Push the wall out",
      "narration": "Slide the wall toward 560 ft. As the curve dives back to the axis the clearance shrinks and eventually the badge flips to 'HITS WALL' — the question is always a comparison of two heights at one x.",
      "state": { "wallPosition": 555, "distanceAlongPath": 555, "wallHeight": 12, "viewMode": "Smooth path" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Same arc, as data",
      "narration": "Switch to sampled points: the smooth path becomes a set of (distance, height) pairs on the grid. The table and the curve carry identical information about the flight.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Sampled points", "distanceAlongPath": 290, "wallPosition": 400, "wallHeight": 12 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Whether a hit is a home run is not about the shape of the curve at all — it is a single yes/no comparison of the parabola's height against the wall's height at the wall's distance.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "White plotting area with faint gray dotted gridlines. X-axis 'Distance (ft)' 0–600 labeled every 100; Y-axis 'Height (ft)' 0–320 labeled at 100/200/300; both axes drawn as black arrows. The trajectory is a thick teal (#1a7f9e) parabola from (0,0) through a vertex near (290, 155) to a landing point near (565, 0), with three or four small teal arrowheads along it pointing in the direction of travel. Black leader lines with serif labels: 'Position of hitter' pointing at the origin from the lower left interior, 'Trajectory of ball' pointing at a small open circle on the rising branch near x=200. A short solid red rectangle sits on the axis at the wall's distance with label 'Outfield wall' above it. Interactive additions: a filled teal dot on the curve at the slider distance with gray dashed lines dropping to the x-axis and across to the y-axis, plus a small callout box reading the ordered pair; a vertical dashed line at the wall distance with a bracket and badge comparing ball height to wall height, green when clearing and red when not. In sampled-points mode the continuous stroke is replaced by ~12 evenly spaced teal dots along the same arc plus a compact two-column table of distance and height values off to the side. Serif typography throughout to match the textbook figure."
}

